 "## Suggested patterns from corpus\n\nThese are concrete EXAMPLES retrieved from the seed corpus based on classification of your task. Each example includes:\n  - WHY the candidate fits (reranker reasoning)\n  - The pattern's prose summary (seed `:summary`)\n  - Capabilities it provides\n  - Proven STRENGTHS — traits observed to work, each with a worked-example DSL snippet you can adapt\n  - Observed WEAKNESSES — failure modes others hit, with the recommended fix\n  - Representative uses where this pattern has shipped\n\nYou always have FOUR moves, and the references below are EVIDENCE for choosing — not a mandate:\n  - ADOPT — use a reference as-is when it is an EXACT fit.\n  - ADAPT — keep a reference's pattern, override the specifics, when it mostly fits.\n  - SPECIALIZE — mint a CHILD of the nearest reference (`mint-behavior!` with `:parent <that behavior-id>`) when the top hit is a BROAD shape rather than an exact fit; the child keeps the proven shape, pins your domain, and accrues evidence under the parent. This is the RECOMMENDED move when a match cleared threshold only on shape.\n  - MINT — mint a fresh behavior (`:parent nil`) when the task is genuinely novel and no reference is a true parent.\nA match clearing threshold does NOT mean adopt/adapt is your only option — weigh each reference's strengths AND its `:avoid-when` against THIS task, then pick the right move. Your job is the RIGHT tree for THIS task; the corpus is evidence, not gospel.\n\n### Structural patterns (top 4 from corpus retrieval)\n#### Top match — Contract-comparison (confidence: 1.00)\nWhy this fits: This candidate perfectly matches the task's structural shape (compare two contracts) and specific requirements (differences, similarities, recommendation). Its recommended pattern uses parallel per-contract extraction into structured vectors, which exactly aligns with the intent to produce a structured comparison with specific keys (:key-differences, :similarities, :recommendation).\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nContract-comparison trees compare two contracts (or two versions) for key differences, similarities, and a recommendation. The proven shape: :parallel per-contract structured-extraction (:output-schemas guaranteeing typed provisions), then sequential synthesis :llm consuming the structured vectors (NOT raw text). Always extract first, then diff — feeding both raw contracts to a single synthesis :llm hits attention limits and induces hallucinated provisions.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - compares two contracts (or two versions of the same contract) for key differences and similarities\n  - produces :key-differences (provision-level diff), :similarities (shared provisions), :recommendation (which version to prefer or what to negotiate)\n  - two-input shape: separate :contract-a and :contract-b blackboard keys, not a single :document\n  - natural fit for :parallel extraction across both contracts followed by sequential diff synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** parallel per-contract extraction reduces wall-time when both contracts can be processed independently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: both contracts share a roughly comparable structure AND the per-contract extraction is identical work\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:contract-a] :writes [:provisions-a] :output-schemas {:provisions-a [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}] [:llm {:reads [:contract-b] :writes [:provisions-b] :output-schemas {:provisions-b [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}]] [:llm {:reads [:provisions-a :provisions-b] :writes [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** diff-synthesis :llm sees TWO large input contexts simultaneously — hits attention/budget limits sooner than single-doc summarization (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: both contracts are >5KB AND the synthesis :llm receives the full text of both directly\n    - Recommended fix: extract structured per-contract provisions first (compact representations), then feed those CONDENSED structures to the synthesis :llm instead of raw text\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without per-contract structured extraction, the synthesis :llm hallucinates provisions that don't exist in either contract (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the synthesis :llm reads raw :contract-a + :contract-b directly without intermediate structured extraction\n    - Recommended fix: always have an intermediate :llm with :output-schemas that extracts a typed provisions vector PER contract before the diff step — the diff :llm consumes the structured vectors, not raw text\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract version-vs-version diff (e.g. provider contract v2.0 vs v3.1 — produces provision-level differences + recommendation on which to prefer)\n  - vendor-contract negotiation review (compare current draft vs prior version)\n  - any two-document structural comparison where provision-level diff matters\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Contract-comparison (confidence: 1.00)\nWhy this fits: This is the fingerprint version of the top-ranked candidate. It shares the exact same content, strengths, and recommended pattern for parallel extraction followed by sequential synthesis, which is the optimal architectural choice for this contract comparison task.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nContract-comparison trees compare two contracts (or two versions) for key differences, similarities, and a recommendation. The proven shape: :parallel per-contract structured-extraction (:output-schemas guaranteeing typed provisions), then sequential synthesis :llm consuming the structured vectors (NOT raw text). Always extract first, then diff — feeding both raw contracts to a single synthesis :llm hits attention limits and induces hallucinated provisions.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - compares two contracts (or two versions of the same contract) for key differences and similarities\n  - produces :key-differences (provision-level diff), :similarities (shared provisions), :recommendation (which version to prefer or what to negotiate)\n  - two-input shape: separate :contract-a and :contract-b blackboard keys, not a single :document\n  - natural fit for :parallel extraction across both contracts followed by sequential diff synthesis\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** parallel per-contract extraction reduces wall-time when both contracts can be processed independently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: both contracts share a roughly comparable structure AND the per-contract extraction is identical work\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:contract-a] :writes [:provisions-a] :output-schemas {:provisions-a [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}] [:llm {:reads [:contract-b] :writes [:provisions-b] :output-schemas {:provisions-b [:vector [:map [:topic :string] [:terms :string]]]}}]] [:llm {:reads [:provisions-a :provisions-b] :writes [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:key-differences :similarities :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** diff-synthesis :llm sees TWO large input contexts simultaneously — hits attention/budget limits sooner than single-doc summarization (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: both contracts are >5KB AND the synthesis :llm receives the full text of both directly\n    - Recommended fix: extract structured per-contract provisions first (compact representations), then feed those CONDENSED structures to the synthesis :llm instead of raw text\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without per-contract structured extraction, the synthesis :llm hallucinates provisions that don't exist in either contract (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the synthesis :llm reads raw :contract-a + :contract-b directly without intermediate structured extraction\n    - Recommended fix: always have an intermediate :llm with :output-schemas that extracts a typed provisions vector PER contract before the diff step — the diff :llm consumes the structured vectors, not raw text\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract version-vs-version diff (e.g. provider contract v2.0 vs v3.1 — produces provision-level differences + recommendation on which to prefer)\n  - vendor-contract negotiation review (compare current draft vs prior version)\n  - any two-document structural comparison where provision-level diff matters\n\n#### Alternative #2 — Comparative summary (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: This candidate is a close fit as it handles multi-source structured comparison (research-then-synthesize). However, its own content explicitly states to use 'contract-comparison' (the top-ranked candidate) when the task is exactly diffing two versions/contracts. It remains highly fit due to the :parallel extraction shape.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nComparative summary is the multi-source research-then-synthesize variant: parallel per-source extraction → :code aggregator structures extractions per source → final :llm synthesizes a structured comparison (common / differences / recommendation). Always use :parallel (not :sequence) for independent per-source stages — wall-time becomes max(per-source). CRITICAL: use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source stages so comparable fields stay aligned. Limit to ~5 sources per synthesis; for more, do hierarchical comparison. Use contract-comparison (parallel-independent task-class) when the structure is exactly diff-two-versions; comparative-summary is for general N-source comparison.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :parallel (or :map-each over sources) per-source :llm extraction → :code aggregator → :llm synthesizer that produces a structured comparison\n  - natural for any 'read N versions/sources and produce a comparison' work — product comparison, contract diff, multi-source fact verification\n  - outputs are typically a structured comparison object (per-source key, common-elements, differences, recommendation)\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :reads include ALL per-source extractions structured as a per-source map — supports apples-to-apples comparison by source rather than free-form prose (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the sources are roughly comparable AND a structured comparison is the value (vs free-form narrative)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]] [:code {:reads [:extract-a :extract-b] :writes [:by-source] :fn (fn [{:keys [extract-a extract-b]}] {:by-source {:a extract-a :b extract-b}})}] [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:common :differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n#### Alternative #3 — Comparative summary (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: Similar to its tree-class counterpart, this fingerprint provides the correct structural shape for multi-source synthesis but is a more general pattern than the specific contract-comparison tree-class.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:sum