:differences :recommendation]}] [:final {:keys [:common :differences :recommendation]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :parallel (NOT :sequence) for the per-source extraction stages when sources are independent — extraction wall-time becomes max(per-source) not sum(per-source) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: sources are independent (no need to read source B before extracting source A)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:parallel [:llm {:reads [:source-a] :writes [:extract-a]}] [:llm {:reads [:source-b] :writes [:extract-b]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form per-source extraction (different :output-schemas per source or open-ended free text) makes the synthesizer's apples-to-apples comparison hard — comparable fields drift across sources (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-source extractions use different schemas OR free-form text\n    - Recommended fix: always use IDENTICAL :output-schemas across per-source extraction stages — comparable fields stay aligned, synthesizer can do direct per-field diff\n\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing more than ~5 sources in a single :llm call exceeds context — the comparison loses fidelity on per-source detail (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: source count >5 AND each source's extraction is non-trivial\n    - Recommended fix: for >5 sources, hierarchical comparison: cluster sources into 2-3 groups → comparative-summary within each group → final cross-group comparison\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - product-feature comparison across 3-4 vendor docs: per-vendor extraction → side-by-side feature matrix\n  - contract diff across 2 versions: per-version extraction → key-differences + similarities + recommendation\n  - multi-source fact verification: per-source claim extraction → common claims + contradictions + confidence\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 1 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Transformation (confidence: 0.80)\n   Why this fits: The task is a 'mechanical reshape of data between deterministic shapes' (structured contract data to a comparison vector/summary). The candidate's recommendation to use :map-each for per-item transformations and the emphasis on declared schemas for deterministic mappings fits the structure of the requested output (vector of key-differences). It also correctly identifies the boundary where judgment (recommendation) starts, though the primary shape is a transformation.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Transformation is mechanical reshape of data between deterministic shapes. ALWAYS use :code (not :llm) for deterministic transformations — preserves fidelity, costs nothing, runs in ms; :llm for transformation produces non-deterministic output. For per-item independent transformations, :map-each isolates per-item failures. ALWAYS declare :output-schemas on transformation output and :input-schemas on downstream :reads so shape mismatch fails at the boundary, not deep in the consumer. Composes into etl-pipeline (sequential transform chain), parallel-sum (map-reduce transformation), sequential-pipeline shells, and map-reduce shells. Avoid for transformations that need judgment (semantic rephrasing / style shift — use :llm) or for unstructured input (extract first).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given input data + a target shape spec, emit the input restructured to the target shape\n  - natural fit anywhere a tree needs to change data shape without semantic interpretation\n  - outputs are typically a single restructured value or a vector of per-item transformations\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** implement transformations in :code (NOT :llm) when the mapping is deterministic — preserves byte-for-byte fidelity, costs zero tokens, runs in milliseconds (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the transformation rule can be expressed as a pure function over the input\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:output] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:output (mapv (fn [r] (select-keys r [:a :b])) (:input inputs))})}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for per-item independent transformations over a vector, use :map-each rather than a single batch :code call when the per-item work is non-trivial — supports per-item failure isolation (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: items can fail independently AND partial success has value\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :records :as :record :writes [:transformed]} [:code {:reads [:record] :writes [:transformed] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:transformed (assoc (:record inputs) :stamp \"x\")})}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** using :llm for a deterministic transformation (CSV → JSON, snake_case → camelCase) produces non-deterministic output AND wastes tokens (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation rule is fully specifiable\n    - Recommended fix: always :code for deterministic transformations; reserve :llm for transformations that need judgment (semantic-rephrasing / style-shift / domain-translation)\n\n  - **Failure mode:** transformations without :output-schemas on the downstream :reads break silently when an upstream change shifts the output shape — the consumer gets garbage instead of an error (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the transformation feeds another stage that binds on specific fields\n    - Recommended fix: declare :output-schemas on the transformation output AND :input-schemas on the downstream :reads — shape mismatch fails loudly at the boundary, not deep in the consumer\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - CSV row → JSON object mapping per-field\n  - extracted entity records → DB-row shape with derived fields\n  - input list → output list with N filters / projections applied\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                      